### Code Review

#### 1. Unhandled Empty State (Critical)
If a customer has never placed an order, `listOrders` will return an empty array `[]`. Consequently, `latest` will be `undefined`, and attempting to access properties on `latest` (`latest.id`, `latest.placedAt`, `latest.lines`) will throw an unhandled runtime error (`TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`).

**Recommendation:**
Handle the empty state and update the return type to `Promise<LatestOrderView | null>`:
```typescript
export async function latestOrder(customerId: string): Promise<LatestOrderView | null> {
  const orders: Order[] = await listOrders(customerId, { sort: "placedAt:desc", limit: 1 });
  const latest = orders[0];
  if (!latest) {
    return null;
  }
  return {
    id: latest.id,
    placedAt: latest.placedAt.toISOString(),
    totalCents: latest.lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.priceCents * line.qty, 0),
    itemCount: latest.lines.reduce((sum, line) => sum + line.qty, 0),
  };
}
```

---

#### 2. Item Count vs. Line Item Count
`itemCount: latest.lines.length` counts distinct line items rather than the total quantity of items ordered. If a customer orders 3 of one item and 2 of another, `latest.lines.length` will report `2` instead of `5`. If the UI intends to show total items purchased, sum `line.qty` instead.

